{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966808,"nonce":"zkzhhy1GfaS2MGomb2zaC8OuBFs=","title":"Web pointer dispatch must cache + merge modifier state, and Modifiers::command needs a","message":"Two coupled bugs that silently broke keyboard combos on web (resolved\n0ba677ef):\n\n1. `dispatch_pending` in `crates/blinc_app/src/web.rs` passed\n   `shift=ctrl=alt=meta=false` to `dispatch_event_full`. Browser\n   mouse / wheel events DO carry modifier flags, they just weren't\n   threaded through. Symptom: canvas-kit Shift+Drag (rubber-band\n   add-to-selection), any widget's Cmd+click chord, every modifier-\n   gated DRAG / POINTER_DOWN handler saw the wrong state.\n\n2. `Modifiers::command()` in `crates/blinc_core/src/events.rs` was\n   cfg'd `#[cfg(target_os = \"macos\")]` → `meta()` else `ctrl()`. On\n   wasm32-unknown-unknown the host OS is unknown at compile time, so\n   `command()` reduced to `ctrl()` even when the user pressed Cmd\n   on a Mac browser. Cmd+Z silently didn't match — node_editor's\n   `handle_key_down` only fires undo / redo / select-all / duplicate\n   on `mods.command()`.\n\n**Fix pattern (lifted from the desktop runner):**\n\n- `WebApp` carries a `cached_modifiers: blinc_platform::Modifiers`\n  field. Every DOM input closure (mousemove / mousedown / mouseup /\n  wheel / keydown / keyup) stamps it from the event's `shift_key()`\n  / `ctrl_key()` / `alt_key()` / `meta_key()` flags BEFORE invoking\n  its dispatch helper. A `stamp_modifiers_from_mouse(app, evt)`\n  helper keeps the writes centralised. WheelEvent inherits from\n  MouseEvent — the cast `evt.as_ref().unchecked_ref::\u003cMouseEvent\u003e()`\n  works, but the inherited modifier methods are also callable\n  directly on `WheelEvent` so an inline literal is fine too.\n\n- `dispatch_pending` reads `app.cached_modifiers` once at entry and\n  forwards `mods.shift / ctrl / alt / meta` into every\n  `dispatch_event_full` call.\n\n- `Modifiers::command()` gains a wasm32 arm returning\n  `meta() || ctrl()`. Accept either modifier on wasm — Cmd on Mac\n  browsers and Ctrl on Windows / Linux browsers both match without\n  needing runtime OS detection (which would mean a navigator/UA\n  sniff in event-path code).\n\n**Why this matters / when to apply:**\n\n- Any new DOM input closure that calls a `dispatch_*` helper must\n  stamp `cached_modifiers` first. Skipping the stamp will silently\n  regress modifier-state for the duration the user holds the key\n  but doesn't trigger keyup.\n\n- The cached field is also the only source of modifier state for\n  events the browser never delivers as keyboard events — e.g. the\n  user tabs into the window with shift already held. Mouse closures\n  refresh on every event so that path stays correct.\n\n- Mirrors the desktop `cached_modifiers` field at\n  windowed.rs `WindowedAppState::cached_modifiers`, which merges\n  `event.shift || cached.shift` per dispatch. The web equivalent\n  doesn't need the OR-merge because mouse events DO carry their own\n  shift flag — we just refresh the cache from every event so by\n  the time `dispatch_pending` reads it, it reflects the live\n  modifier state.\n\n**Don't:** cfg `Modifiers::command` only on `target_os = \"macos\"`.\nThat branches on the COMPILE target, not the RUNTIME platform —\nwasm32-unknown-unknown drops into the non-macos arm regardless of\nwhich OS the user's browser actually runs on.\n\n**Companion fix (904e6e9b): preventDefault on Cmd/Ctrl + letter\nchords.** Before, the keydown closure only called `prevent_default`\nfor printable TEXT_INPUT characters, so the browser still fired\nits own default for Cmd+D (bookmark), Cmd+S (save), Cmd+F (find),\nCmd+P (print), Cmd+A (DOM select-all) etc. on top of the app\nshortcut. Rule used: any `(meta || ctrl) \u0026\u0026 key_code in 0x41..=0x5A`\ngets preventDefault. Letters only — digits stay live so Cmd+1..9\nstill switches browser tabs, and function keys stay live so F1..F12\nstill opens devtools. Clipboard chords (Cmd+C / V / X) are safe to\ninclude because the browser fires `copy` / `cut` / `paste` events\nindependently of the keydown default, and Blinc's paste listener\nattaches to those events directly.","files":null}]}